data: {"id":"chatcmpl-20261017225110460435465c955d568RkHFa6XF","created":1792277471,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_a8aa4c50f4e74cdab69e8e4e","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"file_write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"# Production Planning Model — Version 1\\n\\n**Status:** v1 — initial binding of purpose, data, and decision semantics\\n**Date:** 2026-10-17\\n**Scope:** Weekly production planning for a workshop making two products (A and B)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Purpose\\n\\nDecide how many units of product A and product B the workshop should produce\\nper week so that total weekly profit is as large as possible, while respecting\\nthe workshop's limited machine time and labor capacity.\\n\\n## 2. Data (given)\\n\\n| Item                            | Product A | Product B |\\n|---------------------------------|-----------|-----------|\\n| Machine time (hours per unit)   | 2         | 1         |\\n| Labor time (hours per unit)     | 1         | 3         |\\n| Profit (USD per unit)           | 40        | 50        |\\n\\nCapacities available per week:\\n- Machine time: **100 hours**\\n- Labor: **90 hours**\\n\\n## 3. Decision Variables (in words)\\n\\n- **xA** — the number of units of product A to produce per week.\\n- **xB** — the number of units of product B to produce per week.\\n\\nThese are the quantities the planner is free to choose; everything else\\n(hours consumed, profit earned) follows from them.\\n\\n## 4. Objective (in words)\\n\\nMaximize total weekly profit: $40 earned for every unit of A produced, plus\\n$50 earned for every unit of B produced.\\n\\nIn symbols: **maximize 40·xA + 50·xB** (USD per week).\\n\\n## 5. Constraints (in words)\\n\\n1. **Machine capacity:** total machine time used — 2 hours for each unit of A\\n   plus 1 hour for each unit of B — must not exceed 100 hours per week.\\n2. **Labor capacity:** total labor used — 1 hour for each unit of A plus 3\\n   hours for each unit of B — must not exceed 90 hours per week.\\n3. **Nonnegativity:** production quantities cannot be negative (no negative\\n   production).\\n\\nIn symbols:\\n- 2·xA + 1·xB ≤ 100  (machine hours)\\n- 1·xA + 3·xB ≤ 90   (labor hours)\\n- xA ≥ 0, xB ≥ 0\\n\\n## 6. Assumptions & Modeling Choices (v1)\\n\\n- **Divisibility:** units are treated as continuously divisible (linear\\n  program). If whole units are required, this becomes an integer program —\\n  check after solving whether the LP optimum is already integral.\\n- **Determinism:** all coefficients (processing times, profits, capacities)\\n  are known constants for the planning week.\\n- **Proportionality:** profit and resource consumption scale linearly with\\n  output; no setup costs, batch minimums, or volume discounts.\\n- **Single period:** one week only; inventory, demand ceilings, and carry-over\\n  between weeks are out of scope in v1.\\n- **Unused capacity has no value:** machine/labor hours not used cannot be\\n  banked or sold.\\n\",\"path\":\"model_v1.md\"}"}}]}}]}

